# SRG-APP-000178-AU-002660  -  CAT I
# The Central Log Server must obfuscate authentication information during
# the authentication process so that the authentication is not visible.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000178-AU-002660
group:           V-206481
severity:        high
cat:             CAT I
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-000206
control_rev5:    IA-6

contribution:    Splunk can keep credentials out of view during authentication: it serves
                 the logon over TLS, it can leave the GET-based login endpoint that would
                 put a password in a URL disabled, and it can stop the stored-credentials
                 REST endpoint and the SPL rest command from returning passwords in
                 cleartext.

governs:         limits.conf - Limits on how much work searches and data processing may consume.
governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           web.conf [settings] enableSplunkWebSSL equals true
check:           limits.conf [storage_passwords_masking] view_cleartext_spl_rest equals false
check:           limits.conf [storage_passwords_masking] view_cleartext_allowlist not_equals *
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          web.conf [settings] enableSplunkWebSSL https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] enable_insecure_login https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
retired_check:   web.conf [settings] enable_insecure_login equals false
                 reason: "Whether or not the GET-based '/account/insecurelogin' REST
                         endpoint is enabled. Provides an alternate GET-based authentication
                         mechanism. If 'true', the following url is available:
                         http://localhost:8000/en-US/account/insecurelogin?loginType=splunk&username=noc&password=XXXXXXX.
                         If 'false', only the main /account/login endpoint is available.
                         Default: false"
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Suppression of local echo while a password is typed at a terminal is a
                 function of the operating system's console and of the browser's password
                 field
external:        Any reverse proxy or load balancer in front of Splunk Web must preserve
                 TLS end to end
external:        Credential prompting and masking performed by an LDAP or SAML identity
                 provider's own logon page

citation:        SRG-APP-000178-AU-002660 check text; web.conf.spec:70-73
                 (enableSplunkWebSSL, default false), web.conf.spec:198-205
                 (enable_insecure_login, default false; when true the password travels in
                 the URL), limits.conf.spec:5072-5092 (view_cleartext_allowlist, default
                 "*" meaning cleartext passwords are always returned),
                 limits.conf.spec:5094-5101 (view_cleartext_spl_rest, default true, lets
                 the SPL rest command return cleartext passwords)
finding:         Splunk does not keep authenticators out of view to the extent the product
                 allows: Splunk Web is not on TLS, the insecure GET login endpoint is
                 enabled, or the storage_passwords_masking settings are left at defaults
                 that return stored passwords in cleartext.
